<Project>

  <!--
    Repo-wide .NET 11 / C# 15 pin.  Engineer-mandated 2026-08-27:
    ".NET 11 and C# 15 latest preview ... c#15 opt in approved and mandated".

    🔴 WHY THIS IS A .targets FILE AND NOT Directory.Build.props — MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED.

    The first attempt put <LangVersion>preview</LangVersion> in Directory.Build.props.
    MSBuild imports .props BEFORE the project body, so the fourteen projects in this repo
    that set <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion> themselves simply overwrote it.  Verified:

        dotnet msbuild csharp/glp_il_codec/GlpIlCodec.csproj -getProperty:LangVersion
        -> latest          (the mandate silently did NOT apply)

    Directory.Build.targets is imported AFTER the project body, so this wins.  A mandate
    that loses to a per-project setting is not a mandate.

    🔴 AND WHY 'preview' AND NOT '15' — ALSO MEASURED on SDK 11.0.100-preview.7.26381.103:

        csc /langversion:?  ->  ... 13.0, 14.0 (default), latestmajor, preview, latest

      * There is NO '15' or '15.0' token.  Both <LangVersion>15</LangVersion> and
        <LangVersion>15.0</LangVersion> FAIL with 'error CS1617: Invalid option'.
      * 'latest' is NOT C# 15.  The compiler's own #error version probe reports:
            LangVersion=latest   ->  "Language version: latest (14.0)"
            LangVersion=preview  ->  "Language version: preview"
        So every project left on 'latest' was getting C# 14 despite the mandate.
      * C# 15 is reachable ONLY through 'preview' on this SDK.

    WHEN TO CHANGE: once csc lists a numbered '15.0', switch to 15.0 so the pin stops
    floating with whatever 'preview' happens to mean in a later SDK.

    Per-project opt-OUT is still possible by setting LangVersion after this import, but
    nothing in this repo should do so without an engineer ruling.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <LangVersion>preview</LangVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
